% Dead suspensions are dropped from the store once they outnumber the
% live ones (store compaction). This kills most `item`s, both in bulk
% and one at a time, then checks keyed lookups (`has`, indexed on
% item's argument) and a full scan (`sum_all`) see only the live ones.
:- module(store_compaction, [run/4, sweep_run/1]).

:- chr_constraint item/1, drop/1, has/2, sum_all/2,
    fill/2, drop_range/2, churn/2, run/4, sweep/1, sweep_run/1.

drop(I), item(I) <=> true.

item(I) \ has(I, R) <=> R = true.
has(_, R) <=> R = false.

sum_all(Acc, R), item(I) <=> A is Acc + I, sum_all(A, R).
sum_all(Acc, R) <=> R = Acc.

fill(I, N) <=> I >= N | true.
fill(I, N) <=> item(I), I1 is I + 1, fill(I1, N).

drop_range(I, N) <=> I >= N | true.
drop_range(I, N) <=> drop(I), I1 is I + 1, drop_range(I1, N).

% Tell an item and kill it right away, many times over.
churn(I, N) <=> I >= N | true.
churn(I, N) <=> item(I), drop(I), I1 is I + 1, churn(I1, N).

run(R1, R2, R3, S) <=>
    fill(0, 1000), drop_range(10, 1000), fill(1000, 1010),
    churn(2000, 3000),
    has(5, R1), has(500, R2), has(1005, R3), sum_all(0, S).

% Compaction while a loop over `item` runs. `drop_range` leaves 80 dead
% items and 20 live ones, and stores no item after, so nothing has
% compacted yet. The active `sweep` then walks the items; its first
% firing stores an item, which compacts the store mid-loop. The loop
% must keep its snapshot and still reach items 1..19.
sweep(N) \ item(I) <=> I < N | J is I + N, item(J).

sweep_run(S) <=> fill(0, 100), drop_range(20, 100), sweep(100), sum_all(0, S).
